---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Clearance.astro';
import Console from '../../components/clearance/Console.astro';
import PrCheck from '../../components/clearance/PrCheck.astro';
import ChatApproval from '../../components/clearance/ChatApproval.astro';
import Verify from '../../components/clearance/Verify.astro';
import Closer from '../../components/clearance/Closer.astro';
import MarkAnatomy from '../../components/clearance/MarkAnatomy.astro';
import { u } from '../../lib/url';
const c = (p = '') => u(`clearance/${p}`);
---
<Layout title="Clearance" description="Clearance gates every change to production on your own policy, binds the approval to the exact rendered change that lands, and keeps a record anyone can verify.">
  <section class="hero-c">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <a class="pill" href={c('design-partners/')}><span class="tag">Now boarding</span>Three design-partner pilots, from November 2026 <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <h1 class="display">Let the agents ship. <em>Clear</em> every change.</h1>
      <p class="sub">Clearance holds every change to a protected production environment until your own policy, or a named human, clears it. The clearance is bound to the exact rendered change that lands, and the whole path goes on a record your auditor can verify without taking anyone's word for it. A human commit is governed exactly as an agent's is, from day one.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-solid" href={c('design-partners/')}>Request a pilot <span class="arr">→</span></a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={c('product/')}>Take the tour</a>
      </div>
      <div class="stage" data-reveal><Console /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="clr-wrap standards" aria-label="Built on">
    <p>Fits the pipeline you already fly. Nothing to migrate to.</p>
    <div class="std-row">
      <span>GitHub</span><span>Argo CD</span><span>Kyverno</span><span>OPA</span><span>Sigstore</span><span>in-toto</span><span>Infisical</span><span>Traefik</span><span>Backstage</span><span>OpenTelemetry</span><span>Slack</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap" data-reveal>
      <p class="kicker center" style="text-align:center">The question</p>
      <p class="quote-c mt-2">When an AI agent produces a change that reaches production, can you prove who or what started it, which controls <em>authorized</em> it, who approved it, and what actually deployed?</p>
      <p class="quote-by">Most teams can see the pull request and the reviewer. The chain from intent to cluster is the part nobody holds.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">The thesis</p>
        <h2 class="h2">The control is agent-agnostic. The <em>trigger</em> is agentic.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Clearance gates every change on four things: the change, the target, the actor's identity and the policy in force. AI provenance is recorded as a signal with a confidence, never used as the gate. So you install it on the traffic you already have, and the record is a quarter deep by the time the first agent change arrives.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="personas three" data-reveal>
        <div class="persona"><span class="eyebrow">Adopting agents</span><h3>The chain is broken. This repairs it.</h3><p>Agent changes land under a bot identity with no named human in the approval chain. Clearance puts the human, the policy and the exact change back on one record.</p></div>
        <div class="persona"><span class="eyebrow">About to adopt them</span><h3>Install it before the first agent.</h3><p>The most common buyer has no agents in production yet: a platform team that wants them and a security team that won't allow it until change control holds for bots. Clearance is what lets security say yes.</p></div>
        <div class="persona"><span class="eyebrow">Regulated, no agent plans</span><h3>A tighter change control, with a witness.</h3><p>A bound, witnessed change record is worth having whether or not an agent ever touches the pipeline. The agents, when they come, are already covered.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">What you get</p>
        <h2 class="h2">A gate on the change path, and a record that <em>outlives</em> it.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Four pieces drop onto the GitOps route you already run. Routine changes clear in under a second. Risky ones wait for the right person. Every one lands on the record.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bento">
        <article class="tile span-3" data-reveal>
          <p class="kicker">Provenance detector</p>
          <h3>Who started it, with an honest confidence.</h3>
          <p>Every pull request and commit is tagged confirmed agent, probable agent, confirmed human or unknown. Unknown never quietly becomes human.</p>
          <div class="art">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 420 150" aria-hidden="true">
              <g font-size="12" fill="var(--muted)">
                <text x="0" y="22">Confirmed agent</text><text x="0" y="56">Probable agent</text><text x="0" y="90">Confirmed human</text><text x="0" y="124">Unknown</text>
              </g>
              <g>
                <rect x="130" y="10" width="280" height="16" rx="4" fill="var(--paper-2)"/><rect x="130" y="10" width="196" height="16" rx="4" fill="var(--clr)"/>
                <rect x="130" y="44" width="280" height="16" rx="4" fill="var(--paper-2)"/><rect x="130" y="44" width="72" height="16" rx="4" fill="var(--clr)" opacity="0.6"/>
                <rect x="130" y="78" width="280" height="16" rx="4" fill="var(--paper-2)"/><rect x="130" y="78" width="118" height="16" rx="4" fill="var(--sky)" opacity="0.75"/>
                <rect x="130" y="112" width="280" height="16" rx="4" fill="var(--paper-2)"/><rect x="130" y="112" width="30" height="16" rx="4" fill="var(--accent)"/>
              </g>
              <text x="168" y="124" font-size="11" class="mono" fill="var(--accent)">TREATED AS AGENT IN PROD</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="tile span-3" data-reveal>
          <p class="kicker">Clearance controller</p>
          <h3>A clearance is bound to what renders.</h3>
          <p>A signed, short-lived grant names the rendered manifest tree, every object in it, the target, the policy version and the approver. Change one object after approval and nothing admits it.</p>
          <div class="art">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 420 150" aria-hidden="true">
              <rect x="10" y="20" width="210" height="112" rx="10" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--rule-strong)"/>
              <text x="26" y="44" font-size="11" class="mono" fill="var(--clr)">CLEARANCE TOKEN</text>
              <g font-size="11.5" fill="var(--muted)" class="mono">
                <text x="26" y="66">tree    sha256:e71d…</text>
                <text x="26" y="84">objects 2 · 1 changed</text>
                <text x="26" y="102">policy  7e2d · tier 2</text>
                <text x="26" y="120">expires 60 min</text>
              </g>
              <path d="M220 76 H282" stroke="var(--clr)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>
              <circle cx="220" cy="76" r="4" fill="var(--clr)"/>
              <rect x="282" y="46" width="128" height="60" rx="10" fill="var(--clr-soft)" stroke="var(--clr)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="346" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5" fill="var(--ink)">manifest tree</text>
              <text x="346" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" class="mono" fill="var(--muted)">e71d…a40c</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="tile span-2" data-reveal>
          <p class="kicker">Bypass detection</p>
          <h3>The side door shows up too.</h3>
          <p>Cluster state is diffed against cleared changes. A direct apply becomes a named exception, not a mystery.</p>
          <div class="art">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 260 120" aria-hidden="true">
              <g>
                {Array.from({ length: 30 }, (_, i) => (
                  <rect x={10 + (i % 10) * 24} y={10 + Math.floor(i / 10) * 34} width="18" height="26" rx="3"
                    fill={i === 17 ? 'var(--bad)' : 'var(--clr)'} opacity={i === 17 ? 1 : 0.25 + ((i * 7) % 5) * 0.1} />
                ))}
              </g>
              <circle cx="187" cy="57" r="20" fill="none" stroke="var(--bad)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="tile span-2" data-reveal>
          <p class="kicker">Outage policy</p>
          <h3>Fail the way you decided.</h3>
          <p>An outage never blocks a routine deploy and always holds a secret rotation, unless you choose otherwise. Every degraded minute is recorded.</p>
          <div class="art">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 260 120" aria-hidden="true">
              {['Tier 0', 'Tier 1', 'Tier 2', 'Tier 3'].map((t, i) => (
                <g transform={`translate(0 ${8 + i * 28})`}>
                  <text x="6" y="15" font-size="12" fill="var(--muted)">{t}</text>
                  <rect x="70" y="2" width="44" height="18" rx="9" fill={i < 2 ? 'var(--clr)' : 'var(--paper-2)'} stroke="var(--rule-strong)"/>
                  <circle cx={i < 2 ? 105 : 79} cy="11" r="7" fill="var(--raised)" stroke="var(--rule-strong)"/>
                  <text x="128" y="15" font-size="11" class="mono" fill={i < 2 ? 'var(--clr)' : 'var(--accent)'}>{i < 2 ? 'ALLOW + RECORD' : 'HOLD'}</text>
                </g>
              ))}
            </svg>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="tile span-2" data-reveal>
          <p class="kicker">Evidence record</p>
          <h3>Verified, or only claimed.</h3>
          <p>Each field says whether Clearance checked it or an integration asserted it. Uncertainty made visible beats a claim of completeness.</p>
          <div class="art">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 260 120" aria-hidden="true">
              {[['approver', 'verified'], ['rendered tree', 'verified'], ['agent header', 'claimed'], ['commit sig', 'verified']].map(([k, v], i) => (
                <g transform={`translate(0 ${6 + i * 28})`}>
                  <rect x="4" y="0" width="252" height="22" rx="5" fill="var(--paper)" stroke="var(--rule)"/>
                  <text x="14" y="15" font-size="11.5" class="mono" fill="var(--ink)">{k}</text>
                  <text x="246" y="15" text-anchor="end" font-size="10.5" class="mono" fill={v === 'verified' ? 'var(--clr)' : 'var(--accent)'}>{v.toUpperCase()}</text>
                </g>
              ))}
            </svg>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">How a change gets cleared</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Taxi, hold short, cleared, <em>logged</em>.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="steps" data-reveal>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">01 · TAXI</span><h3>Propose</h3><p>An agent or a person opens a pull request. The GitHub App reads the provenance and posts a check.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">02 · HOLD SHORT</span><h3>Classify</h3><p>Path and resource rules, the environment and CI set the tier. Kyverno or OPA gives the policy verdict.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">03 · CLEARED</span><h3>Clear</h3><p>Policy clears routine tiers in milliseconds. Sensitive ones wait for a named human who didn't start the change.</p></div>
        <div class="step"><span class="n">04 · LOGGED</span><h3>Land and record</h3><p>Argo CD renders the change and admission checks every object against its grant. The outcome is recorded and witnessed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-2"><a href={c('how-it-works/')}>How it works, end to end →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap"><MarkAnatomy /></div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="feature" data-reveal>
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">In the pull request</p>
          <h2 class="h2">The tier, the reason, and the <em>one person</em> who can clear it.</h2>
          <ul class="points">
            <li><b>Plain-language reasons.</b> The check says why a change is tier 2, which rule fired and what it is bound to.</li>
            <li><b>Built on GitHub's own gate.</b> Clearance registers as a deployment protection rule, so GitHub asks before it deploys.</li>
            <li><b>No self-clearance.</b> The proposing agent never satisfies a human clearance, and the approver is never the person who started it.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <PrCheck />
      </div>
      <div class="feature flip" data-reveal>
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Where on-call already lives</p>
          <h2 class="h2">One tap to clear. <em>Never</em> a Friday blocked by the tool itself.</h2>
          <ul class="points">
            <li><b>Chat approvals</b> with the diff, the policy verdict and the expiry right in the card.</li>
            <li><b>Identity through your IdP.</b> The record keeps the OIDC subject and issuer, never a display name alone.</li>
            <li><b>Two humans on critical changes,</b> one from outside the proposing team. Never automatic.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <ChatApproval />
      </div>
      <div class="feature" data-reveal>
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">For the auditor</p>
          <h2 class="h2">Check the record without taking <em>our</em> word for it.</h2>
          <ul class="points">
            <li><b>Hashes, witnessed.</b> Your records stay on your storage. The ledger holds only their hashes, and independent witnesses refuse any history shorter than the last one they saw.</li>
            <li><b>Open verification.</b> <code>clearance verify</code> is open source, needs no access to Clearance, and names the exact line if one was altered.</li>
            <li><b>Exceptions up front.</b> Every packet states out-of-band changes, break-glass and degraded mode, plus what was not covered.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-2"><a href={c('ledger/')}>Evidence and the ledger →</a></p>
        </div>
        <Verify />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">Risk tiers</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Routine traffic flows. Critical changes <em>hold</em>, by design.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Four defaults, tuned per app in a <code>clearance.yaml</code> beside the Argo application. The tier comes from what changed in the rendered objects, not from the size of the YAML edit, and declared intent never lowers it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ladder" data-reveal>
        <div class="rung" style="--c: var(--good)"><span class="lv">Tier 0<b>Routine</b></span><p>Dev and preview environments; docs, tests, non-prod config.</p><span class="who">Auto-clear by policy</span><span class="lat">&lt; 1 s<small>latency</small></span></div>
        <div class="rung" style="--c: var(--sky)"><span class="lv">Tier 1<b>Standard</b></span><p>Prod app code, image bumps with CI green, a log level in a ConfigMap.</p><span class="who">Policy, one reviewer notified</span><span class="lat">&lt; 1 s<small>latency</small></span></div>
        <div class="rung" style="--c: var(--accent)"><span class="lv">Tier 2<b>Sensitive</b></span><p>Replicas, Helm values that change behaviour, feature flags, network policy.</p><span class="who">One named human, owning team</span><span class="lat">Minutes<small>latency</small></span></div>
        <div class="rung" style="--c: var(--bad)"><span class="lv">Tier 3<b>Critical</b></span><p>Secrets, RBAC, IAM, admission policies, Argo Applications, migrations.</p><span class="who">Two named humans, never auto</span><span class="lat">Hours<small>by design</small></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">Who it's for</p>
        <h2 class="h2">One gate, four people who each get <em>what they need</em>.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="personas" data-reveal>
        <div class="persona"><span class="eyebrow">VP Engineering</span><h3>Let the agents ship. Pass the audit anyway.</h3><p>One invoice instead of a quarter of engineer time spent stitching evidence together.</p></div>
        <div class="persona"><span class="eyebrow">Platform lead</span><h3>A gate that fits your GitOps.</h3><p>A Helm install in under an hour, Argo and Kyverno integration, and an open controller you can read.</p></div>
        <div class="persona"><span class="eyebrow">GRC lead</span><h3>Evidence mapped to your controls.</h3><p>A sample packet you can read in five minutes and forward without rework, with a coverage statement.</p></div>
        <div class="persona"><span class="eyebrow">Auditor</span><h3>Verify it yourself. Then sign.</h3><p>Free tooling, a witness you run, and a one-click acceptance that signs a receipt into the ledger. Never a fee on the auditor's side.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="sec-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker amber">Where it stands</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Honest about what's <em>built</em>.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Every Hangar product labels its claims. Clearance does too.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="status-band" data-reveal>
        <div><span class="chip built">Built</span><p>The runtime gateway, in its own <code>clearance</code> repository since October 29, 2026 and running on the Hangar dev cluster: tool tiers T0 to T3, path and field scope, narrow-only limits, CEL deny rules R001 to R020 with a fail-closed R000, a Hangar pack for the Glidepath and Airframe tools, MCP over HTTP with cluster and OIDC sign-in, a Postgres store, PR provenance verdicts, a human inbox, and a hash-chained audit with an anchored checkpoint. Tested without a cluster.</p></div>
        <div><span class="chip draft">Draft</span><p>The two-boundary design, with the runtime gateway as an AuthZEN decision point behind Traefik, plus the deployment gate. The evidence record schema and the token format, now with the security-impact and back-out fields PCI DSS 6.5.1 asks for per change.</p></div>
        <div><span class="chip proposal">Proposal</span><p>The deployment gate (render-time binding and admission policy), gate providers, the recorder, attestations, the witnessed ledger, the audit packet, the control graph, and the assurance providers with their acceptance receipts. Design partners shape these first.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Closer />
</Layout>
